Sure! Here's a step-by-step guide to creating a simple To-Do List app using React:

1. Set up the project:

First, make sure you have Node.js installed on your computer. Then, use the following command to create a new React app:


```bash
npx create-react-app todo-app
```
Navigate to the project folder and start the development server:


```bash
cd todo-app
npm start
```
1. Create components:

In the `src` folder, create the following components:

* `TodoList.js`: This component will display the list of to-dos.
* `TodoItem.js`: This component will display an individual to-do item.
* `AddTodo.js`: This component will handle adding new to-dos to the list.
1. Create the `TodoList` component:

In `TodoList.js`, create a functional component that receives an array of to-dos as a prop and maps them to `TodoItem` components:


```javascript
import React from 'react';
import TodoItem from './TodoItem';

const TodoList = ({ todos }) => {
 return (
 <ul>
 {todos.map((todo, index) => (
 <TodoItem key={index} todo={todo} />
 ))}
 </ul>
 );
};

export default TodoList;
```
1. Create the `TodoItem` component:

In `TodoItem.js`, create a functional component that receives a `todo` object as a prop and displays the to-do's text:


```javascript
import React from 'react';

const TodoItem = ({ todo }) => {
 return <li>{todo.text}</li>;
};

export default TodoItem;
```
1. Create the `AddTodo` component:

In `AddTodo.js`, create a functional component that uses the `useState` hook to manage the input value and a callback function to add a new to-do:


```javascript
import React, { useState } from 'react';

const AddTodo = ({ onAdd }) => {
 const [inputValue, setInputValue] = useState('');

 const handleSubmit = (e) => {
 e.preventDefault();
 if (inputValue.trim()) {
 onAdd(inputValue);
 setInputValue('');
 }
 };

 return (
 <form onSubmit={handleSubmit}>
 <input
 type="text"
 value={inputValue}
 onChange={(e) => setInputValue(e.target.value)}
 />
 <button type="submit">Add</button>
 </form>
 );
};

export default AddTodo;
```
1. Modify the `App` component:

In `App.js`, import the `useState` hook, the `TodoList`, and the `AddTodo` components. Use the `useState` hook to manage the list of to-dos, and pass them as props to the `TodoList` and `AddTodo` components:


```javascript
import React, { useState } from 'react';
import TodoList from './TodoList';
import AddTodo from './AddTodo';

const App = () => {
 const [todos, setTodos] = useState([]);

 const addTodo = (text) => {
 setTodos([...todos, { text }]);
 };

 return (
 <div>
 <h1>To-Do List</h1>
 <TodoList todos={todos} />
 <AddTodo onAdd={addTodo} />
 </div>
 );
};

export default App;
```
